<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Flora Tristan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a>–<a href="/years/1844">1844</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Flora Tristán</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Flora Tristan was a French-Peruvian writer and socialist activist who argued that the progress of women's rights was bound up with the progress of the working class. Her account of a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1833–1834, Peregrinations of a Pariah, appeared in 1838. In 1843 she published The Workers' Union and toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to win workers to her plan for a universal union of working men and women, across the barriers of trade and region; she died suddenly in 1844. In 1848 French workers placed a monument on her tomb.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flora-tristan.jpg" alt="Flora Tristan" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jules Laure · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flora_Tristan_par_Jules_Laure.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
